<BaseLayout
  title="Cache-Aware Dynamic Outlier Offloading (CADO): Precision on a Budget — Shubhojit"
  description="A reflective, mathematically grounded research hypothesis on allocating precision and fast memory dynamically during inference."
>
  <article>
    <div class="meta">AI Systems · Quantization · Inference</div>
    <h1>Cache-Aware Dynamic Outlier Offloading (CADO): Precision on a Budget</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A model does not need every number to be equally precise. But can it know
      which numbers deserve the extra care, and can the hardware afford it?
      Explore the <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CADO repository on GitHub</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a small, recurring bargain inside neural-network inference. We
      would like every computation to be accurate, and every computation to be
      fast. In practice, these wishes compete for the same finite resources:
      bits, bandwidth, cache capacity, and time. We compress representations so
      that more work can fit close to the processor. Then we spend some of the
      savings recovering accuracy where compression hurts.
    </p>

    <p>
      The difficult part is deciding where that care should go. A large number
      looks important, but magnitude is not the same as influence. A value can
      be numerically unusual and still have little effect on the final answer;
      a small value can matter a great deal after it passes through the rest of
      the network. And even a good numerical decision can become a poor systems
      decision if it requires expensive data movement.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cache-Aware Dynamic Outlier Offloading (CADO) starts with that tension.
      The hypothesis is that activation information can help allocate a limited
      high-precision budget more intelligently than a fixed policy. The aim is
      not to preserve every value. It is to ask which values are worth
      preserving under a real resource constraint.
    </p>

    <p class="quiet-note">
      This is a research question, not a performance claim. The prototype below
      is a starting point for making the question concrete; it does not yet
      establish a cache-aware speedup.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem is not just fewer bits</h2>
    <p>
      Quantization is often introduced as a change of representation: replace
      floating-point values with a smaller set of integers, and recover an
      approximation when needed. That description is correct, but incomplete
      for systems work. A representation also determines how data is packed,
      how much can remain close to the compute units, which kernels can be
      used, and how much overhead is paid to move between representations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Imagine a limited shelf beside a workbench. The shelf is fast to reach,
      but it is small. You cannot put every tool on it, so you keep the tools
      you expect to need most. The analogy becomes less comfortable when the
      tools change with every job: yesterday's best selection may be wrong for
      today's task. Dynamic precision routing asks whether a computation can
      make a similar choice about its values, without spending more time
      choosing than it saves by choosing well.
    </p>

    <h2>The mathematical motivation: precision has a price</h2>
    <p>
      Consider one linear layer. Let <strong>X</strong> be its activation
      matrix and <strong>W</strong> its weight matrix:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`Y = XW`}
      label="The output Y of a linear layer is the product of its activation matrix X and weight matrix W."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Each row of <strong>X</strong>
      represents an input activation vector, and <strong>W</strong> maps those
      vectors into the layer's output space. Matrix multiplication combines
      them to produce <strong>Y</strong>. This is the computation whose result
      will change when we represent its inputs with less precision.
    </p>

    <p>
      If we quantize both matrices, we no longer compute exactly the same
      product. Write their quantized versions as <strong>X + ΔX</strong> and
      <strong>W + ΔW</strong>, where the delta terms represent quantization
      error. The resulting output difference is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\hat{Y} &= (X + \Delta X)(W + \Delta W) \\ \hat{Y} - Y &= \Delta X W + X \Delta W + \Delta X \Delta W\end{aligned}`}
      label="Expanding the quantized matrix product shows activation error, weight error, and an interaction term."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>ΔX</strong> and
      <strong>ΔW</strong> are the changes introduced by quantizing activations
      and weights. The output error has three parts: activation error carried
      through the weights, weight error applied to the activations, and an
      interaction between both errors. So the impact of a quantized value
      depends on the computation around it, not just on its own error.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a deep network, these effects continue through later layers. That is
      why “keep the largest values” is a plausible starting rule, but not a
      complete theory of importance. Magnitude gives us a cheap signal. It
      does not directly tell us how much a perturbation changes the output,
      the loss, or the task decision. A more principled allocator would ideally
      estimate the cost of lowering precision, not just the size of the value.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a small perturbation to the output, a first-order approximation
      gives a related intuition. If the task loss is <strong>ℒ</strong> and
      the output changes by <strong>δY</strong>, then:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\delta \mathcal{L} \approx \left\langle \nabla_Y \mathcal{L}, \delta Y \right\rangle`}
      label="For a small output perturbation, the first-order change in task loss is approximately the inner product of the loss gradient and the output change."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> <strong>∇<sub>Y</sub>ℒ</strong>
      describes how the loss changes with the output, and <strong>δY</strong>
      is the perturbation caused by reduced precision. Their inner product
      estimates the loss change when that perturbation is small enough for a
      local, first-order approximation to be useful. A large numerical error
      can be harmless in an insensitive direction; a smaller one can be costly
      if it pushes the output toward a bad decision.
    </p>
    <p>
      This does not mean we can cheaply calculate the exact importance of
      every value at inference time. It means magnitude should be treated as a
      proxy to test, rather than as importance itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now add the systems constraint. Let <strong>z<sub>i</sub> = 1</strong>
      mean computation unit <em>i</em> gets the higher-precision path, and
      <strong>z<sub>i</sub> = 0</strong> mean it stays on the lower-precision
      path. Let <strong>T(z)</strong> be computation latency,
      <strong>T<sub>route</sub>(z)</strong> the time spent selecting, packing,
      and dispatching units, <strong>M(z)</strong> the fast-memory working
      set, and <strong>E(z)</strong> the expected task-quality loss. A
      simplified allocation problem is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\min_{z} \quad & T(z) + T_{\mathrm{route}}(z) \\ \text{subject to} \quad & M(z) \le C \\ & E(z) \le \varepsilon \\ & z_i \in \{0,1\}\end{aligned}`}
      label="Choose binary precision allocations to minimize computation and routing latency while respecting fast-memory capacity and a maximum quality loss."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Choose which units receive extra
      precision so that total latency, including routing, is low. The
      fast-memory working set must stay within capacity <strong>C</strong>,
      and expected quality loss must stay below tolerance
      <strong>ε</strong>. The binary variable <strong>z<sub>i</sub></strong>
      records the precision choice for each unit. The routing process has a
      cost; it cannot be treated as free.
    </p>
    <p>
      In an idealized version, each unit would have a known benefit, memory
      cost, and time cost. In a real model, those quantities interact: cache
      behavior is not perfectly additive, kernels have preferred shapes, and
      one routing decision can change the efficiency of another. The equation
      is a map of the problem, not a claim that the problem has already been
      solved.
    </p>

    <p>
      The question becomes more interesting here. We are not only asking how
      to reduce numerical error. We are asking how to spend a scarce resource
      on the errors that matter, while accounting for the cost of discovering
      them.
    </p>

    <h2>The hypothesis</h2>
    <p>
      Under a fixed on-chip memory budget, activation-aware precision routing
      may achieve a better quality–latency tradeoff than a static outlier
      policy. In this framing, the level-one (L1) cache is one possible budget; other
      hardware may expose a different fast-memory tier or capacity.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <strong>π</strong> denote a routing policy,
      <strong>T(π)</strong> its end-to-end latency,
      <strong>Q(π)</strong> its task quality, and <strong>M(π)</strong> its
      fast-memory working set. The research goal can be written as:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\min_{\pi} \quad & T(\pi) \\ \text{subject to} \quad & Q(\pi) \ge Q_{\min} \\ & M(\pi) \le C\end{aligned}`}
      label="Find a routing policy with minimum end-to-end latency while meeting minimum task quality and fast-memory capacity constraints."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Among policies that meet the
      minimum acceptable task quality <strong>Q<sub>min</sub></strong> and
      fit within fast-memory capacity <strong>C</strong>, choose the one with
      the lowest end-to-end latency. A policy that is faster but misses either
      constraint does not count as a solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central question is whether information about the current activation
      distribution can guide the allocation of scarce precision and fast-memory
      resources better than a fixed rule can.
    </p>

    <h2>What would be routed?</h2>
    <p>
      A layer could divide its computation into units such as channels or
      blocks. An allocator would estimate which units are most sensitive to
      reduced precision for the current input, then spend a limited
      high-precision budget on those units. The rest would use a lower-precision
      path.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact signal and granularity remain open design choices. Activation
      magnitude is easy to measure, but magnitude alone need not predict
      quantization error or downstream task quality. Routing could adapt for
      every input, operate at batch or block granularity, or use statistics
      gathered during calibration. These choices trade responsiveness against
      the cost of measuring, packing, and dispatching the selected values.
    </p>

    <h2>A toy prototype</h2>
    <p>
      One concrete starting point is to flag activation columns whose maximum
      magnitude across a batch exceeds a threshold. Let <strong>X</strong> be
      an activation matrix and let <strong>M</strong> be a mask selecting the
      outlier columns. A compact approximation is:
    </p>
    <Equation
      expression={String.raw`Y \approx X_{\mathrm{out}}W_{\mathrm{out}} + Q(X_{\mathrm{normal}})Q(W_{\mathrm{normal}})`}
      label="Approximate the matrix product with a higher-precision contribution from selected outlier columns and a quantized contribution from the remaining columns."
    />
    <p class="math-translation">
      <strong>Translating the math:</strong> Split the matching inner
      dimensions of activation matrix <strong>X</strong> and weight matrix
      <strong>W</strong> into selected outlier columns and the remaining
      columns. Multiply the outlier subset in higher precision; quantize the
      normal subset with <strong>Q</strong> and multiply it in lower precision.
      Add the contributions to approximate <strong>Y</strong>. The
      approximation sign matters: quantization introduces error, and
      identifying outliers does not guarantee that those values are the most
      important ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this first sketch, the routing signal is deliberately simple: a
      column is selected if any activation in the batch crosses a threshold.
      It makes the decomposition tangible, but it is not evidence that the
      threshold identifies the right values or that the resulting computation
      is faster.
    </p>
    <p>
      The public prototype is available in the
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading/tree/master" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CADO repository</a>.
    </p>

    <pre><code>{`import numpy as np


def cado_matrix_multiply(x, weights, threshold=6.0):
    """Toy implementation of a simple CADO-style decomposition.

    Idea:
      1. Identify columns whose max magnitude exceeds a threshold.
      2. Compute those columns in full precision.
      3. Quantize the remaining columns to int8 and reconstruct them.
      4. Add the two contributions back together.

    This is a correctness-oriented sketch, not a cache-aware kernel.
    """
    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float32)
    weights = np.asarray(weights, dtype=np.float32)

    if x.ndim != 2 or weights.ndim != 2 or x.shape[1] != weights.shape[0]:
        raise ValueError("Expected compatible 2D activation and weight matrices")
    if x.shape[0] == 0 or x.shape[1] == 0 or weights.shape[1] == 0:
        raise ValueError("Matrices must have non-empty dimensions")

    # Step 1: identify outlier columns for this batch.
    outlier_mask = np.max(np.abs(x), axis=0) >= threshold
    result = np.zeros((x.shape[0], weights.shape[1]), dtype=np.float32)

    # Step 2: keep the selected subset in a dense, higher-precision path.
    if np.any(outlier_mask):
        result += x[:, outlier_mask] @ weights[outlier_mask, :]

    # Step 3: quantize the remaining columns to int8 and reconstruct the product.
    normal_mask = ~outlier_mask
    if np.any(normal_mask):
        x_normal = x[:, normal_mask]
        w_normal = weights[normal_mask, :]

        scale_x = max(float(np.max(np.abs(x_normal))) / 127.0, 1e-12)
        scale_w = max(float(np.max(np.abs(w_normal))) / 127.0, 1e-12)

        x_int8 = np.clip(np.round(x_normal / scale_x), -127, 127).astype(np.int8)
        w_int8 = np.clip(np.round(w_normal / scale_w), -127, 127).astype(np.int8)

        result += (x_int8.astype(np.int32) @ w_int8.astype(np.int32)) * (scale_x * scale_w)

    return result`}</code></pre>

    <p>
      This is a correctness-oriented sketch, not a cache-aware implementation
      or a performance result. The threshold is shared across the batch, the
      high-precision subset is selected by activation magnitude rather than
      measured sensitivity, and the code does not constrain its working set
      to capacity <strong>C</strong>. It also omits kernel-launch, packing,
      and hardware effects. Those are not incidental details. They are part
      of the question CADO is trying to answer.
    </p>

    <h2>What the prototype has shown so far</h2>
    <p>
      I began with a question small enough to fit inside a matrix. If a few
      activation columns contain unusually large values, what happens if I
      spare them the rounding that the rest must endure? Would preserving
      those columns in higher precision bring the result closer to the one
      computed entirely in FP32?
    </p>
    <p>
      In one synthetic experiment, it did. Against the FP32 reference, the
      CADO-style decomposition produced a relative error of about
      <strong>0.0031</strong>, compared with <strong>0.0376</strong> for uniform
      INT8 quantization. The
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading/blob/master/experiments/03_baseline_comparison.py" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">experiment script</a>
      and its
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading/blob/master/results/baseline_comparison.csv" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">recorded results</a>
      are available in the repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is something satisfying about this first result. A small part of
      the computation receives different treatment, and the numerical error
      falls. In a workload constructed with outliers, the intuition seems to
      hold: perhaps precision need not be distributed evenly to be useful.
      Perhaps some values deserve more of it than others.
    </p>
    <p>
      But the experiment answers only the question I put to it. The data was
      synthetic, and relative error measures numerical closeness, not the
      quality of an answer produced by a real model. I have not yet shown that
      the same allocation improves task performance, or that identifying and
      separating the outliers takes less time than it saves. Nor does a smaller
      high-precision subset, by itself, tell me what happens inside the cache.
    </p>
    <p>
      The
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading/blob/master/experiments/05_equal_budget_comparison.py" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">equal-budget experiment</a>
      and
      <a href="https://github.com/shubhojit/CADO---Cache-Aware-Dynamic-Outlier-Offloading/blob/master/experiments/06_cache_tile_sweep.py" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">tile sweep</a>
      are attempts to move a little closer to those questions. They compare
      dynamic and static allocation while limiting the number of columns
      allowed the higher-precision path. Yet a tile size is still only a
      stand-in for memory pressure. It tells me how I have arranged the
      experiment, not how the processor experiences it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A policy can look economical in a matrix and become expensive in a
      kernel. The work of finding the outliers, packing the selected values,
      dispatching the computation, and bringing the results back together may
      consume the very time the policy was meant to save. The hardware has its
      own account of the computation, and I have not yet seen the full ledger.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next experiment, then, needs to leave the comfort of the synthetic
      setup. It should measure the complete cost of routing on real hardware:
      end-to-end latency, packing and dispatch overhead, working-set size, and
      cache counters, all under a controlled memory budget.
    </p>
    <p>
      For now, I want to keep the claim modest. The prototype has shown that,
      under these controlled conditions, <strong>where precision is allocated
      can affect numerical error</strong>. Whether that difference survives
      contact with a real model, a real cache, and the costs of execution
      remains an open question.
    </p>
    <p>
      I find that uncertainty useful. It marks the boundary between what the
      experiment has made visible and what I still need to understand.
    </p>

    <h2>What I still can’t see</h2>
    <p>
      The first results make it tempting to believe the intuition: some values
      seem to benefit from more precision than others. But each result so far
      has come from a world I made deliberately simple, where the outliers are
      easy to plant and the computation fits neatly on a page.
    </p>
    <p>
      I keep returning to the distance between a mask that lowers numerical
      error and a computation that runs better. Magnitude gives me a convenient
      way to choose columns. I don’t yet know whether it finds the values that
      matter most, or simply the ones that are easiest to notice. And the act
      of choosing has a cost of its own: values must be found, gathered,
      packed, and eventually brought back together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cache is harder to reason about from a spreadsheet. A working set may
      fit within a budget and still behave poorly once the processor encounters
      its shape, its tiles, and the rest of the workload. I haven’t yet measured
      that full encounter.
    </p>
    <p>
      So I don’t know where the idea stops helping. Perhaps the signal will be
      too weak, or the routing too costly. Perhaps a simpler static choice will
      turn out to be kinder to the hardware. These possibilities don’t make the
      question feel smaller to me. They remind me what the next experiment has
      to earn.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope and prior work</h2>
    <p>
      Mixed-precision outlier handling is not new. The
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07339" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">“8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale” method, known as LLM.int8() (LLM means large language model)</a>
      isolates outlier features for higher-precision computation.
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10438" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">SmoothQuant, “Accurate and Efficient 8-bit Quantization for Large Language Models”</a>
      uses activation statistics to shift quantization difficulty between
      activations and weights. And
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00978" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Activation-aware Weight Quantization (AWQ)</a>
      uses activation-aware information to guide weight quantization. The
      question here is narrower: can the allocation policy be explicitly
      optimized for a constrained fast-memory budget, and does that improve
      end-to-end quality and latency against relevant baselines?
    </p>

    <h2>A note on token efficiency</h2>
    <p>
      This connects to a broader question about efficient inference, but it is
      not the same question as token efficiency. Shorter sequences reduce how
      much work a model performs. Precision routing asks how efficiently each
      unit of work is represented and moved. One reduces the amount of work;
      the other tries to allocate resources within the work that remains.
      They may complement each other, but they should be measured as separate
      budgets.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the question leaves us</h2>
    <p>
      The appeal of dynamic precision is easy to understand: not every value
      should necessarily receive the same treatment. The harder question is
      whether we can identify the values that matter, cheaply enough for the
      identification to be worthwhile, and in a way the hardware can execute
      efficiently.
    </p>
    <p>
      CADO begins with a modest proposal: use activation information to decide
      where to spend a limited precision budget, then judge the decision by
      the complete system, not by the numerical approximation alone. The
      mathematics explains why the allocation might matter. Only a careful
      comparison can tell us whether it does.
    </p>

